// New articles, 18 November 2026 (part 7): technology.
ADDART({id:"hearingaids",t:"Hearing Aids and Cochlear Implants",c:"Technology",i:"🦻",w:"Hearing aid",r:["ear","signlang","headphones","microphones","prosthetics"],
p:[["Helping people hear","Hearing aids make sounds louder and clearer."],["Bionic ears","Cochlear implants stimulate the hearing nerve directly."],["Now over the counter","Some hearing aids can now be bought without a prescription."]],
l:"Hearing aids are small electronic devices worn in or behind the ear that make sounds louder and clearer for people with hearing loss. A microphone picks up sound, a processor adjusts it for the wearer’s particular pattern of hearing loss, and a tiny speaker delivers it into the ear canal. Before electronics, people used ear trumpets, funnel-shaped horns that gathered sound. Electric hearing aids appeared in the early 20th century, transistor aids small enough to wear on the body arrived in the 1950s, and digital hearing aids, introduced in the 1990s, can reduce background noise and connect wirelessly to phones. For people with severe or profound deafness, cochlear implants can help: they bypass damaged parts of the inner ear and send electrical signals directly to the auditory nerve through electrodes placed in the cochlea. Australian surgeon Graeme Clark implanted the first multichannel cochlear implant in 1978, and more than a million people now use them. The World Health Organization estimates that more than 1.5 billion people live with some hearing loss, but most who need hearing aids do not have them.",
s:[["How hearing aids work","Modern digital hearing aids split sound into frequency bands and amplify each according to the wearer’s hearing test. Directional microphones focus on voices in front, and programs adapt to quiet rooms, restaurants or music. Styles include behind-the-ear, in-the-ear and nearly invisible in-canal aids."],["Cochlear implants","A cochlear implant has an external microphone and processor worn behind the ear, which sends signals through the skin to a receiver implanted under it. The receiver stimulates up to about 22 electrodes in the cochlea. Implants do not restore normal hearing, but with training many users understand speech and use phones. Children born deaf who receive implants early often develop spoken language."],["Over-the-counter aids","In October 2022, the United States allowed hearing aids for mild to moderate hearing loss to be sold over the counter without a prescription, aiming to lower prices. In 2024, Apple added a hearing-aid feature to its AirPods Pro earbuds, approved by US regulators."],["Hearing loss and health","Untreated hearing loss is linked to social isolation, depression and a higher risk of dementia. A 2023 study suggested that hearing aids may slow cognitive decline in older adults at higher risk. Deaf communities emphasize that deafness is also a cultural identity, with sign languages, and that implants are a personal choice."]],
k:[["Hearing aid parts","Microphone, processor, speaker"],["Digital aids","From the 1990s"],["First multichannel cochlear implant","Graeme Clark, 1978"],["Cochlear implant users","More than a million"],["People with hearing loss","More than 1.5 billion (WHO)"]],
d:[["1600s","Ear trumpets used"],["1898","First electric hearing aid"],["1952","Transistor hearing aids introduced"],["1978","First multichannel cochlear implant"],["1996","Fully digital hearing aids launched"],["2022","US allows over-the-counter hearing aids"]],
s2:[["Beethoven’s ear trumpets","Ludwig van Beethoven, who became deaf, used ear trumpets made by Johann Nepomuk Maelzel, the inventor of the metronome. He later communicated mostly through conversation notebooks."],["Hidden hearing aids","In the 19th century, hearing aids were often hidden in fans, hats, canes and even thrones; King John VI of Portugal had a throne with lion-head armrests that funneled sound to him."]],
sp:["Graeme Clark","Graeme Clark was inspired by his father, who was deaf. He developed the multichannel cochlear implant at the University of Melbourne; it was first implanted in Rod Saunders in 1978, and the company Cochlear was founded to produce it."],
g:[["Cochlea","The spiral-shaped hearing organ in the inner ear"],["Auditory nerve","The nerve carrying sound signals to the brain"],["Amplify","To make louder"],["Profound deafness","Very severe hearing loss"]],
f:["More than 1.5 billion people have some hearing loss.","Cochlear implants stimulate the hearing nerve directly.","The US allowed over-the-counter hearing aids in 2022."],
fun:"Some modern hearing aids can stream phone calls and music directly into the ears and translate languages in real time."});

ADDART({id:"ereaders",t:"E-readers",c:"Technology",i:"📖",w:"E-reader",r:["novel","encyclopedias","libraries","print","smartphone"],
p:[["Digital books","E-readers are devices designed for reading electronic books."],["Electronic ink","Their screens look like paper and use little power."],["Changing publishing","E-books changed how people buy and borrow books."]],
l:"An e-reader is a portable electronic device designed mainly for reading digital books, called e-books. Most dedicated e-readers use electronic paper, or e-ink, screens, which reflect light like printed paper instead of glowing like phone screens; they are easy to read in sunlight, cause less eye strain for many readers and use power only when the page changes, so batteries can last weeks. E-ink technology was developed at the Massachusetts Institute of Technology in the 1990s. Early e-readers such as the Rocket eBook (1998) and Sony Reader (2006) had limited success, but Amazon’s Kindle, launched in November 2007 with wireless access to an online bookstore, made e-books popular. E-readers can store thousands of books, let readers change text size, look up words and highlight passages. E-book sales grew rapidly in the early 2010s and then leveled off, with printed books remaining popular. Libraries lend e-books digitally. Debates continue over e-book prices, ownership rights, as buyers usually license rather than own e-books, and whether reading on screens affects understanding.",
s:[["Electronic ink","An e-ink screen contains millions of tiny capsules filled with charged white and black particles in fluid. Electric fields move the particles to the top or bottom, forming text. The image stays without power, which is why some e-readers show a picture even when switched off. Color e-ink screens have become available."],["The Kindle","Amazon’s first Kindle sold out in hours in 2007. Its built-in cellular connection let users download books in under a minute without a computer. By 2010, Amazon was selling more e-books than hardcover books in the United States. Other popular e-readers include Kobo and Barnes & Noble’s Nook."],["Accessibility","E-readers help people with poor eyesight by enlarging text, and some read aloud using text-to-speech. Special fonts can help readers with dyslexia. E-books also make books available in remote places without bookstores."],["Ownership","In 2009, Amazon remotely deleted copies of George Orwell’s 1984 and Animal Farm from customers’ Kindles because of a rights problem, an ironic event that highlighted the fact that e-book buyers typically receive a license controlled by the seller."]],
k:[["Screen type","Electronic paper (e-ink)"],["E-ink developed","MIT, 1990s"],["Kindle launched","November 2007"],["Battery life","Often weeks"],["Library lending","E-books borrowed digitally"]],
d:[["1971","Project Gutenberg begins digitizing free books"],["1997","E Ink Corporation founded"],["1998","Rocket eBook released"],["2006","Sony Reader uses e-ink"],["2007","Amazon launches the Kindle"],["2010","Amazon sells more e-books than hardcovers in the US"]],
s2:[["Project Gutenberg","In 1971, Michael Hart typed the US Declaration of Independence into a university computer and shared it, starting Project Gutenberg, which now offers more than 70,000 free e-books of works in the public domain."],["Paper or screen?","Some studies suggest that readers understand long texts slightly better on paper than on screens, especially when reading for information under time pressure, though the differences are small and depend on the reader and the task."]],
sp:["Audiobooks","Alongside e-books, audiobooks have grown rapidly with smartphones and streaming services. Many people now ‘read’ books by listening while commuting or exercising."],
g:[["E-book","A book in digital form"],["E-ink","A paper-like electronic display"],["License","Permission to use something under conditions"],["Public domain","Works no longer protected by copyright"]],
f:["E-ink screens use power only when the page changes.","The Kindle launched in 2007.","Project Gutenberg began in 1971."],
fun:"Amazon once deleted copies of George Orwell’s 1984 from readers’ devices, an event many found fittingly Orwellian."});

ADDART({id:"videocalls",t:"Video Calls",c:"Technology",i:"📹",w:"Videotelephony",r:["internet","phone","smartphone","covid19","remotework"],
p:[["Seeing while talking","Video calls let people see and hear each other in real time."],["A long-imagined invention","Picturephones were demonstrated as early as 1964."],["The pandemic boom","Video calls became part of daily life during COVID-19."]],
l:"Video calling, or videotelephony, is the real-time transmission of moving images and sound between people in different places, letting them see and hear each other. The idea of a ‘telephone with pictures’ appeared in science fiction in the late 19th century. AT&T demonstrated its Picturephone at the 1964 New York World’s Fair and launched a commercial service in 1970, but it was expensive and few people used it. Businesses adopted videoconferencing systems in the 1980s and 1990s. Video calling became widespread with faster internet, webcams and smartphones: Skype added video calls in 2006, Apple introduced FaceTime in 2010, and apps such as Zoom, Microsoft Teams, Google Meet and WhatsApp followed. During the COVID-19 pandemic in 2020, video calls became essential for work, school, medicine, religious services and keeping in touch with family; Zoom’s daily meeting participants rose from about 10 million in December 2019 to more than 300 million in April 2020. Video calls remain central to remote work, though some people report ‘Zoom fatigue’.",
s:[["How it works","A camera and microphone capture video and sound, which software compresses into a stream of data using codecs. The data travels over the internet to other participants, where it is decompressed and displayed. Delays of more than a fraction of a second make conversations awkward, so video calls need fast, stable connections."],["Picturephone","AT&T’s Picturephone had a small black-and-white screen and cost about 160 dollars a month in Pittsburgh in 1970, equivalent to over 1,000 dollars today. It failed commercially because few people had one to call. Decades later, cheap webcams and the internet made the idea practical."],["Pandemic boom","In 2020, schools moved to online classes, doctors offered telehealth appointments, and courts and parliaments met online. Weddings and funerals were streamed. ‘Zoom’ became a verb, and video calls changed expectations about working from home."],["Zoom fatigue","Researchers at Stanford University suggested in 2021 that video calls are tiring because of constant close-up eye contact, seeing oneself on screen, reduced movement and the extra effort of reading facial cues. Turning off self-view and taking breaks can help."]],
k:[["Early demonstration","AT&T Picturephone, 1964"],["Skype video","2006"],["FaceTime","2010"],["Pandemic peak (Zoom)","300 million daily participants (April 2020)"],["Known problem","‘Zoom fatigue’"]],
d:[["1927","Bell Labs demonstrates two-way television"],["1964","Picturephone shown at the New York World’s Fair"],["1970","Picturephone service launched"],["2006","Skype adds video calls"],["2010","Apple introduces FaceTime"],["2020","Video calls boom during the COVID-19 pandemic"]],
s2:[["Video calls in fiction","Science fiction long predicted video phones, from Albert Robida’s ‘telephonoscope’ in the 1880s to the video booth call in 2001: A Space Odyssey (1968) and Dick Tracy’s wristwatch two-way TV."],["Sign language","Video calls have been especially valuable for deaf people, allowing conversations in sign language. Video relay services connect deaf callers with interpreters who relay calls to hearing people."]],
sp:["Calls from space","Astronauts on the International Space Station hold video calls with family, schools and mission control, sometimes with delays of a few seconds as signals travel via satellites."],
g:[["Codec","Software that compresses and decompresses data"],["Webcam","A camera connected to a computer"],["Telehealth","Health care delivered remotely"],["Latency","Delay in data transmission"]],
f:["The Picturephone was demonstrated in 1964.","Zoom grew to 300 million daily participants in 2020.","Video calls help deaf people talk in sign language."],
fun:"In 1927, US Commerce Secretary Herbert Hoover appeared in an early two-way television demonstration between Washington and New York."});

ADDART({id:"fuelcells",t:"Fuel Cells",c:"Technology",i:"🔋",w:"Fuel cell",r:["hydrogen","battery","ev","apollo","energytransition"],
p:[["Electricity from hydrogen","Fuel cells turn hydrogen and oxygen into electricity, water and heat."],["No burning","They make power through a chemical reaction, not combustion."],["From Apollo to buses","Fuel cells powered spacecraft and now run some vehicles."]],
l:"A fuel cell is a device that converts the chemical energy of a fuel, usually hydrogen, directly into electricity through a reaction with oxygen, producing only water and heat as byproducts. Unlike a battery, it does not run down or need recharging; it keeps producing electricity as long as fuel and oxygen are supplied. Welsh scientist William Grove demonstrated the principle in 1839 with his ‘gas battery’. Fuel cells were first used practically in the 1960s on NASA’s Gemini and Apollo spacecraft, where they provided electricity and drinking water for astronauts. Today they power some buses, trucks, forklifts, trains and cars, such as the Toyota Mirai and Hyundai Nexo, as well as backup power for data centers and hospitals. Fuel cells are efficient and quiet, but their environmental benefits depend on how the hydrogen is made: most hydrogen today comes from natural gas, releasing carbon dioxide, while ‘green’ hydrogen made with renewable electricity is still costly. Battery electric vehicles have outpaced fuel cell cars, partly because hydrogen fueling stations are scarce.",
s:[["How it works","A fuel cell has two electrodes, an anode and a cathode, separated by an electrolyte. Hydrogen at the anode splits into protons and electrons. The protons pass through the electrolyte, while the electrons travel through an external circuit, providing electric current, before combining with oxygen and protons at the cathode to form water. Many cells are stacked together for more power."],["Types","Proton-exchange membrane fuel cells, used in vehicles, work at low temperatures and start quickly. Solid oxide fuel cells operate at high temperatures and are used for stationary power. Alkaline fuel cells powered the Apollo spacecraft."],["Fuel cells in space","Apollo’s fuel cells produced electricity and water for the crew. On Apollo 13 in 1970, an oxygen tank explosion disabled the fuel cells’ oxygen supply, forcing the astronauts to use the lunar module as a lifeboat. Space Shuttle fuel cells also produced drinking water."],["Fuel cell vehicles","Hydrogen cars can refuel in a few minutes and travel 500 kilometers or more, but there are only around a thousand hydrogen stations worldwide, mostly in Japan, South Korea, China, Germany and California. Fuel cells may be more useful for heavy trucks, ships and trains."]],
k:[["Fuel","Usually hydrogen"],["Byproducts","Water and heat"],["Invented","William Grove, 1839"],["First practical use","NASA spacecraft, 1960s"],["Example cars","Toyota Mirai, Hyundai Nexo"]],
d:[["1839","William Grove demonstrates a gas battery"],["1932","Francis Bacon develops an alkaline fuel cell"],["1965","Fuel cells power the Gemini 5 spacecraft"],["1969","Apollo fuel cells supply power and water on the Moon missions"],["2014","Toyota Mirai fuel cell car launched"],["2018","Hydrogen fuel cell trains enter service in Germany"]],
s2:[["Hydrogen trains","In 2018, Germany introduced the world’s first passenger trains powered by hydrogen fuel cells, the Coradia iLint, on routes without overhead electric lines. Some operators later chose battery trains instead because of hydrogen costs."],["Francis Thomas Bacon","British engineer Francis Thomas Bacon, a descendant of the philosopher Francis Bacon, spent decades developing practical alkaline fuel cells. His designs were licensed for the Apollo program, and President Nixon reportedly told him, ‘Without you, Tom, we wouldn’t have gotten to the Moon.’"]],
sp:["Water for astronauts","The water produced by Apollo’s fuel cells was used for drinking and preparing food, but it sometimes contained hydrogen bubbles, which gave astronauts gas."],
g:[["Electrolyte","A substance that lets ions move between electrodes"],["Anode and cathode","The two electrodes of a cell"],["Green hydrogen","Hydrogen made using renewable electricity"],["Combustion","Burning"]],
f:["Fuel cells produce only water and heat.","Apollo astronauts drank water made by fuel cells.","The first hydrogen passenger trains ran in Germany in 2018."],
fun:"The principle of the fuel cell was discovered in 1839, decades before the internal combustion engine became practical."});

ADDART({id:"exoskeletons",t:"Exoskeletons",c:"Technology",i:"🦿",w:"Powered exoskeleton",r:["robot","prosthetics","nervous","olymp","ai"],
p:[["Wearable robots","Exoskeletons are wearable frames that support or strengthen the body."],["Walking again","Some help people with paralysis stand and walk."],["Lighter work","Workers use exoskeletons to reduce strain when lifting."]],
l:"An exoskeleton, in technology, is a wearable device that fits around the body and supports, protects or strengthens its movements. The name comes from the hard outer skeletons of insects and crabs. Powered exoskeletons use motors, sensors and batteries to move joints such as the hips and knees, while passive exoskeletons use springs and counterweights. Medical exoskeletons help people with spinal cord injuries, strokes or muscle diseases stand, walk and exercise; the US Food and Drug Administration first approved one, the ReWalk, for personal use in 2014. In factories and warehouses, exoskeletons support workers’ backs and shoulders during lifting or overhead work, reducing fatigue and injuries. Armies have tested exoskeletons to help soldiers carry heavy loads. Consumer exoskeletons for hiking were launched in the 2020s. Exoskeletons remain expensive and heavy, and researchers are developing soft ‘exosuits’ made of fabric and cables. Science fiction, from Iron Man to the power loader in the film Aliens, has long imagined them.",
s:[["Medical exoskeletons","A medical exoskeleton straps to the legs and torso. Sensors detect when the user shifts weight or leans, and motors move the hips and knees in a walking pattern. Users often need crutches for balance. Standing and walking can improve circulation, bone health and wellbeing."],["Brain-controlled walking","At the opening of the 2014 FIFA World Cup in Brazil, a paralyzed young man named Juliano Pinto used a brain-controlled exoskeleton developed by Miguel Nicolelis’s team to kick a ball. In 2019, a French man with tetraplegia controlled an exoskeleton using brain implants."],["At work","Passive exoskeletons that support arms during overhead work are used by car manufacturers. Back-support exoskeletons help warehouse workers and nurses lift. Studies show they can reduce muscle strain, though they must fit well and not shift loads to other parts of the body."],["The history","In 1890, Russian inventor Nicholas Yagn patented a device with springs to help soldiers walk and jump. General Electric’s Hardiman, built in the 1960s, was a huge powered suit meant to lift 680 kilograms, but it proved too heavy and unstable."]],
k:[["Types","Powered and passive"],["First FDA-approved personal exoskeleton","ReWalk, 2014"],["Medical use","Helping paralyzed people stand and walk"],["Industrial use","Supporting backs and shoulders"],["Early patent","Nicholas Yagn, 1890"]],
d:[["1890","Nicholas Yagn patents a walking aid"],["1965","General Electric begins the Hardiman project"],["2011","ReWalk used in rehabilitation clinics"],["2014","Brain-controlled exoskeleton at the World Cup; ReWalk approved for home use"],["2019","Man with tetraplegia controls an exoskeleton with brain implants"]],
s2:[["Soft exosuits","Researchers at Harvard developed soft exosuits made of fabric, straps and cables driven by small motors, which assist walking with less weight and bulk. They may help stroke patients and reduce the energy needed for walking and running."],["Exoskeletons in fiction","Iron Man’s armor, the power loader in Aliens (1986) and the mech suits of Edge of Tomorrow show exoskeletons granting superhuman strength. Real devices are much more modest."]],
sp:["Ekso Bionics","Amanda Boxtel, paralyzed in a skiing accident in 1992, became one of the first people to walk using an Ekso exoskeleton in 2010 and became an advocate for the technology."],
g:[["Exoskeleton","A hard outer frame"],["Tetraplegia","Paralysis of all four limbs"],["Passive device","One without motors"],["Rehabilitation","Recovery of abilities after injury"]],
f:["Exoskeletons can help paralyzed people walk.","The first personal exoskeleton was approved in 2014.","GE built a huge powered suit in the 1960s."],
fun:"General Electric’s 1960s Hardiman exoskeleton weighed about 680 kilograms, as much as the load it was meant to lift."});

ADDART({id:"maglev",t:"Maglev Trains",c:"Technology",i:"🚄",w:"Maglev",r:["highspeedrail","rail","magnet","chinacountry","japancountry"],
p:[["Floating trains","Maglev trains float above their tracks using magnets."],["Record speeds","A Japanese maglev reached 603 km/h in 2015."],["Few lines","Only a handful of maglev lines operate, mostly in Asia."]],
l:"A maglev, short for magnetic levitation, train floats above a guideway using powerful magnets instead of running on wheels and rails. Magnets also propel and guide the train, using a linear electric motor built into the track. With no wheel friction, maglevs can travel very fast, smoothly and quietly. The idea was patented in the early 20th century, and German engineer Hermann Kemper patented a maglev design in 1934. The first commercial maglev was a slow airport shuttle in Birmingham, England, opened in 1984. The Shanghai Maglev, opened in 2004 using German Transrapid technology, links Pudong Airport to the city at up to 300 kilometers per hour, having run at 430 kilometers per hour for years. Japan’s superconducting SCMaglev set the world speed record for trains of 603 kilometers per hour in 2015. It is planned for the Chuo Shinkansen between Tokyo and Nagoya, but construction delays, including a long dispute over a tunnel in Shizuoka resolved in 2026, have pushed the opening to 2034 or later. Maglevs are costly to build and incompatible with existing railways, limiting their use.",
s:[["How maglev works","Germany’s Transrapid used electromagnets on the train that wrap under the guideway and pull upward, keeping it about 1 centimeter above the track. Japan’s SCMaglev uses superconducting magnets cooled to very low temperatures, which repel coils in the guideway walls and lift the train about 10 centimeters at speed; it runs on rubber wheels at low speeds."],["Shanghai Maglev","The Shanghai Maglev covers 30 kilometers in about seven to eight minutes. It was the world’s first high-speed commercial maglev. A planned extension to Hangzhou was cancelled after residents protested about cost and possible electromagnetic effects."],["Speed records","On 21 April 2015, a seven-car SCMaglev test train reached 603 kilometers per hour on the Yamanashi test line in Japan, the fastest speed ever for a train. China has tested maglevs designed for 600 kilometers per hour."],["Chuo Shinkansen","The Chuo Shinkansen is planned to link Tokyo and Nagoya in about 40 minutes and later Osaka, mostly through tunnels. Originally targeted for 2027, it was delayed by Shizuoka Prefecture’s concerns about water flow from a tunnel under the Southern Alps. The governor approved construction in July 2026."]],
k:[["Stands for","Magnetic levitation"],["Speed record","603 km/h (Japan, 2015)"],["First commercial maglev","Birmingham airport, 1984"],["Shanghai Maglev opened","2004"],["Chuo Shinkansen opening","2034 or later"]],
d:[["1934","Hermann Kemper patents a maglev design"],["1984","Birmingham airport maglev opens"],["2004","Shanghai Maglev opens"],["2006","Transrapid test crash in Germany kills 23"],["2015","SCMaglev reaches 603 km/h"],["2026","Shizuoka approves Chuo Shinkansen tunnel"]],
s2:[["The Lathen accident","In September 2006, a Transrapid test train crashed into a maintenance vehicle on the test track at Lathen, Germany, killing 23 people. The accident, caused by human error, contributed to Germany abandoning plans for domestic maglev lines."],["Hyperloop","The hyperloop concept, popularized by Elon Musk in 2013, would send pods through low-pressure tubes using magnetic levitation. Several companies tested prototypes, but most shut down, and no passenger hyperloop has been built."]],
sp:["Urban maglevs","Slower maglevs serve cities in Asia, including the Linimo line in Aichi, Japan, opened for Expo 2005, and lines in Changsha and Beijing in China and Incheon in South Korea."],
g:[["Levitation","Floating in the air"],["Linear motor","An electric motor that produces straight-line motion"],["Superconductor","A material with no electrical resistance when very cold"],["Guideway","The track a maglev follows"]],
f:["Maglev trains float above their tracks.","A Japanese maglev reached 603 km/h in 2015.","The Shanghai Maglev opened in 2004."],
fun:"Passengers on the Shanghai Maglev can watch a display showing the train’s speed as it zooms toward 300 kilometers per hour."});

ADDART({id:"syntheticfibers",t:"Nylon and Synthetic Fibers",c:"Technology",i:"🧵",w:"Synthetic fiber",r:["plastics","clothing","weaving","fossilfuels","microplastics"],
p:[["Man-made threads","Synthetic fibers such as nylon and polyester are made from chemicals."],["Nylon stockings","Nylon stockings caused shopping frenzies in the 1940s."],["Most of our clothes","Synthetics now make up most of the world’s fiber production."]],
l:"Synthetic fibers are textile fibers made by people from chemicals, mostly derived from oil and natural gas, rather than from plants or animals. The first fully synthetic fiber, nylon, was invented by Wallace Carothers and his team at the DuPont company in 1935. Nylon was strong, elastic and silky, and when nylon stockings went on sale in the United States in May 1940, millions of pairs were sold within days. During World War II, nylon was diverted to parachutes, ropes and tents. Other synthetics followed: polyester, invented in Britain in 1941; acrylic; and spandex, or elastane, introduced in 1958 and sold as Lycra. Today synthetic fibers, led by polyester, make up about two-thirds of global fiber production, used in clothing, sportswear, carpets, ropes, seat belts and industrial materials. They are cheap, durable and quick-drying, but they are made from fossil fuels, shed microplastic fibers when washed and are hard to recycle, adding to plastic pollution. Fast fashion has greatly increased their use.",
s:[["How synthetic fibers are made","Chemicals are joined into long chain molecules called polymers. The polymer is melted or dissolved and forced through tiny holes in a spinneret, like a showerhead, forming filaments that are cooled, stretched and wound into yarn."],["Wallace Carothers","Carothers led DuPont’s research into polymers, producing neoprene, a synthetic rubber, in 1930 and nylon in 1935. Despite his success, he struggled with depression and died in 1937 before nylon went on sale."],["Nylon riots","Nylon stockings were so popular that, after wartime shortages, crowds of thousands queued and scuffled for them in American cities in 1945 and 1946, events newspapers called ‘nylon riots’."],["Environmental problems","Each wash of synthetic clothing can release hundreds of thousands of microfibers, which pass through wastewater plants into rivers and seas. Polyester takes centuries to break down. Recycled polyester, mostly from plastic bottles, and fiber-catching washing machine filters are partial solutions."]],
k:[["First fully synthetic fiber","Nylon, 1935"],["Inventor","Wallace Carothers (DuPont)"],["Most produced fiber","Polyester"],["Share of world fiber","About two-thirds"],["Problem","Microplastic shedding"]],
d:[["1935","Nylon invented at DuPont"],["1940","Nylon stockings go on sale in the US"],["1941","Polyester invented in Britain"],["1945–1946","‘Nylon riots’ in American cities"],["1958","Spandex invented"],["1965","Kevlar invented by Stephanie Kwolek"]],
s2:[["Kevlar","In 1965, DuPont chemist Stephanie Kwolek invented Kevlar, a fiber five times stronger than steel by weight, used in bulletproof vests, helmets, tires and space equipment. It has saved thousands of police officers’ lives."],["Where the name came from","DuPont said ‘nylon’ was a made-up word. A popular myth claims it combines New York and London, but the company has denied this."]],
sp:["Velcro and nylon","George de Mestral’s hook-and-loop fastener became practical only when he discovered that nylon, sewn under infrared light, formed tough, tiny hooks."],
g:[["Polymer","A long chain molecule made of repeating units"],["Spinneret","A device that forms fibers from liquid polymer"],["Microfiber","A tiny fiber shed from fabric"],["Fast fashion","Cheap clothing made quickly in large amounts"]],
f:["Nylon was invented in 1935.","Polyester is the most produced fiber.","Synthetic clothes shed microplastics when washed."],
fun:"About 4 million pairs of nylon stockings were sold in a few hours on the first day they went on sale nationally in the United States."});

ADDART({id:"bluetooth",t:"Bluetooth",c:"Technology",i:"🦷",w:"Bluetooth",r:["wifi","headphones","smartphone","radio","smarthome"],
p:[["Short-range wireless","Bluetooth connects devices over short distances without cables."],["Named after a king","It is named after Harald Bluetooth, a Viking king."],["Billions of devices","Earbuds, keyboards, watches and cars use Bluetooth."]],
l:"Bluetooth is a wireless technology standard for exchanging data over short distances, typically up to about 10 meters, using radio waves in the 2.4 gigahertz band. It connects phones to wireless earbuds, speakers, smartwatches, keyboards, mice, game controllers, cars, fitness trackers and medical devices. Bluetooth was developed by the Swedish company Ericsson starting in 1989 as a wireless replacement for cables, and the Bluetooth Special Interest Group, formed in 1998 by Ericsson, IBM, Intel, Nokia and Toshiba, manages the standard. The first consumer Bluetooth device, a hands-free mobile phone headset, appeared in 1999. Bluetooth uses ‘frequency hopping’, rapidly switching between 79 channels to avoid interference. Bluetooth Low Energy, introduced in 2010, lets small devices such as trackers and sensors run for months or years on tiny batteries. The name comes from Harald ‘Bluetooth’ Gormsson, a 10th-century king who united Danish tribes, just as the technology unites devices. More than 5 billion Bluetooth devices are shipped each year.",
s:[["How it works","Bluetooth devices form small networks called piconets, in which one device connects to several others. Pairing creates a secure link, often by confirming a code. Frequency hopping, up to 1,600 times a second, reduces interference from Wi-Fi and microwave ovens, which use the same band."],["The name and logo","Engineer Jim Kardach suggested the name ‘Bluetooth’ in 1997 as a temporary code name after reading a historical novel about Vikings. The logo combines the runes for H and B, Harald Bluetooth’s initials. Harald may have been nicknamed for a dead tooth that looked blue."],["Bluetooth Low Energy","Bluetooth Low Energy powers item trackers, heart-rate monitors, smart locks and contact-tracing apps used during the COVID-19 pandemic. Bluetooth 5, from 2016, increased range and speed, and newer versions support audio sharing to several listeners at once."],["Security","Bluetooth vulnerabilities have occasionally allowed attackers to intercept data or take control of devices, so keeping devices updated matters. Item trackers have raised concerns about stalking, leading manufacturers to add alerts for unknown trackers traveling with a person."]],
k:[["Range","Typically up to about 10 m"],["Frequency","2.4 GHz band"],["Developed by","Ericsson (from 1989)"],["Low Energy version","2010"],["Named after","Harald Bluetooth, Danish king"]],
d:[["1989","Ericsson begins work on short-range radio"],["1998","Bluetooth Special Interest Group founded"],["1999","First Bluetooth headset released"],["2010","Bluetooth Low Energy introduced"],["2016","Bluetooth 5 released"]],
s2:[["Harald Bluetooth","Harald Gormsson ruled Denmark and parts of Norway in the late 10th century. He is remembered for uniting Danish tribes and introducing Christianity, recorded on the Jelling runestones, a UNESCO World Heritage Site."],["Hedy Lamarr’s legacy","The frequency-hopping technique used in Bluetooth echoes a 1942 patent by actress Hedy Lamarr and composer George Antheil for torpedo guidance."]],
sp:["Audio sharing","Bluetooth LE Audio and Auracast technology, introduced in the 2020s, let one device broadcast sound to many earbuds or hearing aids at once, for example in airports, theaters and gyms."],
g:[["Pairing","Linking two Bluetooth devices securely"],["Frequency hopping","Rapidly switching radio channels"],["Piconet","A small Bluetooth network"],["Rune","A letter of an ancient Germanic alphabet"]],
f:["Bluetooth is named after a Viking king.","The Bluetooth logo combines two runes.","Bluetooth hops channels up to 1,600 times a second."],
fun:"Bluetooth’s name was meant to be temporary until marketers found something better, but no better name was agreed on."});

ADDART({id:"keyboards",t:"Computer Keyboards and Mice",c:"Technology",i:"⌨️",w:"Computer keyboard",r:["typewriter","comp","touchscreens","videogames","spine"],
p:[["Typing and pointing","Keyboards and mice are the main ways people control computers."],["QWERTY’s long life","The QWERTY layout dates back to typewriters of the 1870s."],["Douglas Engelbart’s mouse","The computer mouse was demonstrated in 1968."]],
l:"Computer keyboards and mice are input devices that let people type text and control computers. Keyboards descend from the typewriter: the QWERTY layout, named after the first six letters on the top row, was designed by Christopher Latham Sholes for typewriters sold from 1874 and remains the standard for English despite alternatives such as the Dvorak layout. Early computers used punched cards and teletype terminals, and keyboards connected to screens became standard in the 1970s. The computer mouse was invented by Douglas Engelbart and Bill English at the Stanford Research Institute in the 1960s; Engelbart showed it publicly in 1968 in a famous presentation later called ‘The Mother of All Demos’. The mouse became widespread with graphical interfaces, especially Apple’s Macintosh in 1984. Optical mice replaced ball mice around 2000, and wireless keyboards and mice are now common. Touchscreens and voice assistants have reduced the role of keyboards and mice on phones, but they remain essential for work, writing and gaming. Mechanical keyboards have a dedicated following.",
s:[["QWERTY","A popular story says QWERTY was designed to slow typists down to prevent jams. Historians think Sholes arranged letters partly to separate common letter pairs that jammed his machine and partly based on telegraph operators’ needs. Once people learned it, switching became hard, an example of ‘path dependence’."],["Other layouts","France and Belgium use AZERTY, Germany QWERTZ. The Dvorak layout, patented in 1936, places the most common letters on the home row, but studies disagree about how much faster it is. Languages such as Chinese and Japanese use input methods that convert typed sounds or strokes into characters."],["The mouse","Engelbart’s first mouse was a wooden box with two metal wheels. The name came from its tail-like cable. Xerox PARC developed it further in the 1970s, and Apple licensed ideas for the Lisa and Macintosh. Optical mice track movement with a tiny camera and LED."],["Health","Long hours of typing and mouse use can contribute to repetitive strain injuries, including carpal tunnel syndrome. Ergonomic keyboards, vertical mice, good posture and breaks can help."]],
k:[["Standard layout","QWERTY"],["QWERTY designer","Christopher Latham Sholes"],["Mouse inventors","Douglas Engelbart and Bill English"],["Mouse demonstrated","9 December 1968"],["Mouse made popular","Apple Macintosh, 1984"]],
d:[["1874","QWERTY typewriter goes on sale"],["1936","Dvorak keyboard patented"],["1964","Engelbart and English build the first mouse"],["1968","‘The Mother of All Demos’"],["1984","Macintosh popularizes the mouse"],["1999","Optical mice reach consumers"]],
s2:[["The Mother of All Demos","On 9 December 1968, in San Francisco, Engelbart demonstrated the mouse, windows, hypertext links, video conferencing and collaborative editing in a 90-minute presentation that predicted much of modern computing."],["Mechanical keyboards","Mechanical keyboards use individual switches under each key, giving a distinct feel and sound. Gamers, programmers and writers have revived interest in them, building custom keyboards with colorful keycaps."]],
sp:["The @ and # keys","The @ symbol was on typewriters for accounting. The # key, called the hash, pound or number sign, gained new life with phone menus and, from 2007, social media hashtags."],
g:[["QWERTY","The standard English keyboard layout"],["Input device","A device used to give information to a computer"],["Graphical interface","A screen with windows, icons and pointers"],["Repetitive strain injury","Injury from repeated movements"]],
f:["QWERTY dates back to 1874.","The first mouse was made of wood.","The mouse was first demonstrated in 1968."],
fun:"Engelbart’s team called the mouse a ‘mouse’ because the cord coming out of the back looked like a tail; nobody remembered who first used the name."});

ADDART({id:"supercomputers",t:"Supercomputers",c:"Technology",i:"🖥️",w:"Supercomputer",r:["comp","chip","ai","weatherforecast","quantumcomp"],
p:[["The fastest computers","Supercomputers perform quintillions of calculations per second."],["Exascale era","Several now exceed one exaflop."],["Science machines","They simulate weather, climate, nuclear physics and medicines."]],
l:"A supercomputer is a computer with performance far beyond that of ordinary computers, used for problems requiring enormous numbers of calculations. Supercomputer speed is measured in floating-point operations per second (FLOPS); the fastest systems now exceed an exaflop, a billion billion calculations per second. The first machines called supercomputers, designed by Seymour Cray, appeared in the 1960s, starting with the CDC 6600 in 1964; the Cray-1 of 1976 became an icon. Modern supercomputers contain tens of thousands of processors and graphics processing units (GPUs) working in parallel, connected by fast networks and cooled by liquid. They are used to forecast weather and model climate, simulate nuclear weapons without testing, design aircraft, study earthquakes and the universe, develop medicines and materials, and train artificial intelligence. The TOP500 list ranks the world’s fastest systems twice a year. In June 2026, China’s LineShine, at the National Supercomputing Centre in Shenzhen, became number one, the first Chinese system at the top since 2017, and it stayed first in November 2026, ahead of the US El Capitan.",
s:[["Measuring speed","TOP500 rankings use the LINPACK benchmark, which measures how fast a computer solves a large set of linear equations. Critics note that it does not reflect all real tasks, especially AI workloads, so other lists measure energy efficiency and AI performance. Some powerful AI systems built by companies are not submitted to the TOP500."],["Exascale","The US Frontier, at Oak Ridge National Laboratory, became the first official exascale supercomputer in May 2022. El Capitan, at Lawrence Livermore National Laboratory, took first place in November 2024. By November 2026, five systems on the list exceeded one exaflop."],["Power and cooling","A top supercomputer may use 20 to 30 megawatts of electricity, as much as a small town. Liquid cooling carries heat away from chips, and engineers compete on the Green500 list for energy efficiency."],["Uses","Weather services run supercomputers to make forecasts several times a day. During the COVID-19 pandemic, supercomputers simulated the virus and screened possible drugs. Scientists model galaxy formation, protein folding and fusion reactors."]],
k:[["Speed unit","FLOPS"],["Exaflop","10¹⁸ calculations per second"],["First supercomputer","CDC 6600, 1964"],["No. 1 (November 2026)","LineShine, China (about 2.2 exaflops)"],["No. 2","El Capitan, USA (about 1.8 exaflops)"]],
d:[["1964","CDC 6600, the first supercomputer"],["1976","Cray-1 released"],["1993","First TOP500 list"],["1996","ASCI Red passes a teraflop"],["2008","Roadrunner passes a petaflop"],["2022","Frontier becomes the first exascale supercomputer"],["2026","China’s LineShine takes first place"]],
s2:[["Seymour Cray","Seymour Cray, often called the father of supercomputing, designed the CDC 6600 and founded Cray Research. His Cray-1 had a distinctive C-shaped design with a padded bench around it, nicknamed the world’s most expensive love seat."],["Supercomputers and chess","IBM’s Deep Blue, a specialized supercomputer, defeated world chess champion Garry Kasparov in 1997. Today, chess programs on ordinary computers are far stronger than any human."]],
sp:["Your phone vs. a Cray","A modern smartphone is many thousands of times faster than the Cray-1, which performed about 160 million calculations per second and cost millions of dollars."],
g:[["FLOPS","Floating-point operations per second"],["Exascale","Capable of at least one exaflop"],["GPU","A processor that handles many calculations in parallel"],["Benchmark","A standard test of performance"]],
f:["The fastest supercomputers exceed an exaflop.","China’s LineShine was ranked No. 1 in 2026.","The first supercomputer was the CDC 6600."],
fun:"An exaflop computer can do in one second what would take every person on Earth calculating once a second for almost four years."});

ADDART({id:"rfid",t:"RFID and NFC",c:"Technology",i:"📶",w:"Radio-frequency identification",r:["qrcodes","digitalpay","radio","dataprivacy","pets","barcodes"],
p:[["Tags that talk","RFID tags store information that can be read by radio waves."],["Tap to pay","NFC, a kind of RFID, lets phones and cards pay with a tap."],["Everywhere","RFID tracks goods, pets, passports and toll payments."]],
l:"Radio-frequency identification (RFID) is a technology that uses radio waves to identify and track tags attached to objects, animals or people. An RFID tag contains a tiny chip and an antenna; when a reader sends out a radio signal, a passive tag uses the signal’s energy to power itself and reply with its stored information, without a battery or a direct line of sight. Active tags have their own batteries and longer range. RFID grew from World War II ‘identify friend or foe’ systems that recognized aircraft by radio. Today it is used in shop security tags, warehouse inventory, library books, toll-road payment, building access cards, public transport cards, microchips implanted in pets and electronic passports. Near-field communication (NFC), developed in the early 2000s, is a short-range form of RFID that works within a few centimeters and lets smartphones and bank cards make contactless payments, share data and act as keys. RFID has raised privacy concerns because tags can sometimes be read without the owner knowing.",
s:[["How it works","Passive RFID tags can be as small as a grain of rice. Low-frequency tags work over centimeters, high-frequency tags over about a meter and ultra-high-frequency tags over several meters, allowing whole pallets of goods to be scanned at once as they pass through a warehouse door."],["Contactless payments","Contactless bank cards and phone wallets use NFC with encryption so that each payment uses a one-time code. Contactless payments spread rapidly in the 2010s and especially during the COVID-19 pandemic, when people avoided touching payment terminals."],["Pets and animals","Pet microchips, inserted under the skin, store an identification number that vets and shelters can scan to reunite lost animals with owners. Farmers use RFID ear tags to track livestock, and scientists tag fish and birds to study migration."],["Privacy","Critics worried that RFID tags in clothing or passports could let people be tracked. Electronic passports use encryption and shielded covers. A few people have chosen to have RFID chips implanted in their hands to open doors and pay, a practice that remains rare."]],
k:[["Stands for","Radio-frequency identification"],["Passive tags","No battery, powered by the reader"],["NFC range","A few centimeters"],["Uses","Payments, passports, pets, inventory, tolls"],["Origin","World War II aircraft identification"]],
d:[["1940s","‘Identify friend or foe’ radio systems"],["1973","First RFID patents"],["1990s","RFID toll collection spreads"],["2004","NFC Forum founded"],["2006","Electronic passports introduced in the US"],["2014","Apple Pay launches NFC phone payments"]],
s2:[["The Thing","In 1945, Soviet schoolchildren presented the US ambassador in Moscow with a carved Great Seal of the United States. Hidden inside was ‘The Thing’, a passive listening device invented by Léon Theremin that was powered by radio waves beamed from outside, a forerunner of RFID. It was discovered only in 1952."],["Barcodes and RFID","Unlike barcodes, RFID tags can be read without being seen and many at once, and they can store more information. They are more expensive, so barcodes remain common on everyday products."]],
sp:["Smart shelves","Some clothing stores use RFID tags on every item so that staff can count stock in minutes and customers can use self-checkout by placing items in a bin that reads all the tags at once."],
g:[["Tag","A small device with a chip and antenna"],["Reader","A device that sends and receives radio signals"],["NFC","Near-field communication"],["Contactless","Working without physical contact"]],
f:["Passive RFID tags have no battery.","NFC works within a few centimeters.","Pet microchips use RFID."],
fun:"Some RFID tags are so small that they can be embedded in paper, such as concert tickets and banknote experiments."});

ADDART({id:"printers",t:"Printers",c:"Technology",i:"🖨️",w:"Computer printer",r:["print","printing3d","comp","paper","typewriter"],
p:[["Paper from computers","Printers turn digital documents into marks on paper."],["Laser and inkjet","Most home and office printers use laser or inkjet technology."],["Ink costs","Printer ink is famously expensive."]],
l:"A computer printer is a device that produces text and images on paper or other materials from digital files. Early computer printers of the 1950s and 1960s were line printers and impact printers that struck an inked ribbon against paper, like typewriters; dot-matrix printers, which formed characters from tiny dots, were common in the 1970s and 1980s. Laser printing was invented at Xerox by Gary Starkweather in 1969, and the HP LaserJet of 1984 brought it to offices. Laser printers use a laser to draw an image on a drum with static electricity, attract powdered toner to it and fuse the toner onto paper with heat. Inkjet printers, which spray tiny droplets of ink, became affordable for homes in the late 1980s and 1990s and print color photos well. Printers are often sold cheaply while manufacturers profit from expensive ink cartridges, a ‘razor and blades’ business model that has drawn criticism and lawsuits. As documents went digital, printing declined in many offices, but printers remain common. 3D printers build solid objects layer by layer.",
s:[["Laser printers","A laser beam scans a rotating drum, changing the electric charge where text and images should appear. Toner particles stick to these areas, are transferred to paper and are melted on by a hot fuser, which is why freshly printed pages feel warm. Laser printers are fast and cheap per page for text."],["Inkjet printers","Thermal inkjets heat ink to form a bubble that pushes a droplet out, while piezoelectric inkjets use a crystal that flexes when electricity is applied. Print heads have hundreds of tiny nozzles. Inkjets are used for photos, posters and, in industry, for printing on fabric, packaging and even food."],["Ink prices","By volume, printer ink has been compared to expensive perfumes. Some printers block cheaper third-party cartridges using chips, and companies have faced lawsuits and fines over this. Refillable ink-tank printers have become popular as an alternative."],["Hidden dots","Many color laser printers add tiny, almost invisible yellow dots to pages, encoding the printer’s serial number and the date. This tracking, originally aimed at counterfeiters, was publicized by digital rights groups in the 2000s."]],
k:[["Main types","Laser and inkjet"],["Laser printer invented","Gary Starkweather, Xerox, 1969"],["Laser printer for offices","HP LaserJet, 1984"],["Older type","Dot-matrix"],["Business model","Cheap printers, costly ink"]],
d:[["1953","First high-speed printer for computers"],["1969","Laser printing invented at Xerox"],["1970s","Dot-matrix printers spread"],["1984","HP LaserJet launched"],["1988","Affordable inkjet printers for homes"],["2005","Hidden printer tracking dots publicized"]],
s2:[["The paperless office","Since the 1970s, people have predicted the ‘paperless office’. Paper use in offices actually rose for decades as printers made printing easy, before declining in the 2010s with email, cloud documents and smartphones."],["Printers and the Gutenberg legacy","Johannes Gutenberg’s printing press of the 1450s mass-produced books with movable type. Computer printers brought printing into homes, letting anyone publish documents."]],
sp:["PC LOAD LETTER","The confusing printer error message ‘PC LOAD LETTER’, meaning ‘add letter-size paper’, became a famous joke about baffling technology after it appeared in the 1999 comedy film Office Space."],
g:[["Toner","Powdered ink used in laser printers"],["Inkjet","A printer that sprays ink droplets"],["Dot-matrix","Printing using a grid of dots"],["Fuser","Part of a laser printer that melts toner onto paper"]],
f:["Laser printing was invented in 1969.","Freshly laser-printed pages are warm.","Some color printers add hidden tracking dots."],
fun:"Printer ink has been compared in price to vintage champagne, costing far more per liter than many luxury liquids."});

ADDART({id:"vendingmachines",t:"Vending Machines",c:"Technology",i:"🥤",w:"Vending machine",r:["money","japancountry","atms","digitalpay","foodsafety"],
p:[["Automatic shops","Vending machines sell items automatically when money is inserted."],["Ancient origins","Hero of Alexandria described a coin-operated holy water dispenser."],["Japan’s favorite","Japan has about one vending machine for every 30 people."]],
l:"A vending machine is an automated machine that dispenses items such as snacks, drinks, tickets or other products after a customer inserts money or pays electronically. The earliest known vending machine was described by Hero of Alexandria in the 1st century AD: a coin dropped into a slot at an Egyptian temple pressed a lever that released a measured amount of holy water. Coin-operated machines selling postcards appeared in London in the 1880s, and in 1888 the Thomas Adams Gum Company installed machines selling chewing gum on New York’s elevated railway platforms. Vending machines spread through the 20th century, selling cigarettes, soft drinks, candy and coffee. Japan has one of the highest densities in the world, about 4 million machines, roughly one for every 30 people, selling hot and cold drinks, food, umbrellas and much more. Modern machines accept cards and phone payments, use touchscreens and track sales over the internet. Automated retail now includes machines selling fresh food, electronics and even cars.",
s:[["Hero’s machine","Hero’s holy water dispenser used a coin falling onto a pan attached to a lever. The weight tipped the pan, opening a valve until the coin slid off, so each coin gave the same amount of water. It is one of the earliest known automatic machines."],["Japan","Japan’s vending machines are popular because of low vandalism and crime, high labor costs and a culture of convenience. Many machines sell both hot and cold drinks from the same unit. Some act as emergency supplies, giving free drinks during earthquakes, and some carry Wi-Fi or AED defibrillators."],["How they work","Coin mechanisms check the weight, size and metal content of coins, and bill validators check banknotes with sensors. Spiral coils or robotic arms deliver products. Modern machines report stock and faults remotely so they can be restocked efficiently."],["Unusual machines","Vending machines around the world have sold live bait, fresh pizza made on the spot, books, gold bars, mashed potatoes and even cars, in multi-story ‘car vending’ towers in the United States."]],
k:[["Earliest known","Hero of Alexandria’s holy water dispenser"],["Modern start","1880s"],["Japan’s machines","About 4 million"],["Payment","Coins, notes, cards, phones"],["Unusual items","Pizza, gold, cars"]],
d:[["1st century AD","Hero describes a coin-operated dispenser"],["1883","Postcard vending machines in London"],["1888","Gum machines on New York train platforms"],["1926","Cigarette vending machines spread in the US"],["1960s","Vending machines spread rapidly in Japan"]],
s2:[["The Automat","Automats were restaurants where customers bought food from rows of small glass-door compartments by inserting coins. Horn & Hardart opened the first in Philadelphia in 1902, and they became famous in New York; the last closed in 1991."],["Vending machine dangers","Each year a few people are injured or killed by tipping vending machines over while trying to get stuck items or free products, leading to warning labels on many machines."]],
sp:["Disaster machines","After the 2011 earthquake and tsunami in Japan, some drink machines were programmed to give out free drinks during emergencies, and they display disaster information on electronic signs."],
g:[["Dispense","To give out"],["Bill validator","A device that checks banknotes"],["Automat","A restaurant using food vending compartments"],["Automated retail","Selling goods by machine"]],
f:["The first known vending machine dispensed holy water.","Japan has about 4 million vending machines.","Some vending machines sell cars."],
fun:"Some Japanese vending machines change their prices based on the outside temperature, and others recognize customers’ faces to suggest drinks."});

ADDART({id:"walkietalkies",t:"Walkie-Talkies and Two-Way Radios",c:"Technology",i:"📻",w:"Walkie-talkie",r:["radio","phone","ww2","firefighters","police"],
p:[["Push to talk","Walkie-talkies are handheld radios for talking over short distances."],["Born in wartime","They were developed for soldiers in World War II."],["Still vital","Police, firefighters and workers rely on two-way radios."]],
l:"A walkie-talkie is a portable, handheld two-way radio that lets people talk to each other over short distances without a phone network. Users press a ‘push-to-talk’ button to speak and release it to listen; usually only one person can talk at a time on a channel, which is why radio users say ‘over’ when they finish speaking. Several inventors developed portable two-way radios in the late 1930s, including Canadian Donald Hings and American Al Gross. During World War II, Motorola produced the backpack SCR-300, nicknamed the walkie-talkie, and the handheld SCR-536 ‘handie-talkie’ for soldiers. After the war, two-way radios spread to police, firefighters, taxis, construction sites, ships and aircraft, and simple walkie-talkies became popular toys. Today professional radio systems use digital technology and networks of repeaters for wider coverage, and emergency services rely on them because they work when phone networks fail. Citizens band (CB) and amateur, or ham, radio let members of the public communicate over longer distances.",
s:[["Radio etiquette","Radio users developed procedures to communicate clearly: ‘over’ means ‘I have finished; your turn’, ‘out’ means the conversation is over, ‘roger’ means ‘received’, and ‘wilco’ means ‘will comply’. The NATO phonetic alphabet, from Alfa to Zulu, spells words clearly."],["Inventors","Donald Hings built a portable radio for Canadian bush pilots and soldiers in 1937. Al Gross, a teenager in Ohio, built handheld radios in the late 1930s and later helped invent the pager and citizens band radio. Motorola engineers led by Dan Noble produced military radios in large numbers."],["Emergency communications","When disasters damage phone towers and power lines, two-way radios, repeaters and amateur radio operators keep communications going. Amateur radio volunteers provided vital links after hurricanes such as Katrina in 2005 and Maria in 2017."],["CB radio","Citizens band radio became a craze in the United States in the 1970s, especially among truckers, who used colorful slang such as ‘breaker one-nine’ and ‘smokey’ for police. The 1975 song ‘Convoy’ and films made CB culture famous."]],
k:[["Type","Handheld two-way radio"],["Key feature","Push-to-talk"],["Wartime models","SCR-300, SCR-536 (Motorola)"],["Inventors credited","Donald Hings, Al Gross"],["Users","Police, firefighters, workers, hobbyists"]],
d:[["1937","Donald Hings develops a portable radio"],["1938","Al Gross builds handheld radios"],["1940","Motorola’s SCR-536 handie-talkie"],["1958","Citizens band radio established in the US"],["1970s","CB radio craze in the US"]],
s2:[["Walkie-talkie or handie-talkie?","In World War II, ‘walkie-talkie’ referred to the larger backpack radio, while the handheld unit was a ‘handie-talkie’. After the war, ‘walkie-talkie’ came to mean the handheld type."],["Push-to-talk apps","Smartphone apps such as Zello offer push-to-talk features over the internet, and some phone networks provide push-to-talk services for businesses."]],
sp:["Mayday","The emergency distress call ‘Mayday’ was proposed in 1923 by radio officer Frederick Mockford at Croydon Airport in London, from the French m’aider, ‘help me’. It is repeated three times."],
g:[["Two-way radio","A radio that can send and receive"],["Push-to-talk","Pressing a button to transmit"],["Repeater","A station that rebroadcasts signals to extend range"],["Phonetic alphabet","Code words for letters"]],
f:["Radio users say ‘over’ when they finish speaking.","Walkie-talkies were developed for World War II.","Two-way radios work when phone networks fail."],
fun:"The word ‘roger’ comes from the old phonetic alphabet, in which R was ‘Roger’, standing for ‘received’."});
